<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanishka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 127–150</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Kanishka the Great, Kanishka I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kanishka was the most famous ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>, reigning in the 2nd century CE, probably from about 127, over lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>, with capitals at Purushapura (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabatak-inscription">Rabatak inscription</a> records his descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises">Kujula Kadphises</a> and his use of Bactrian for official purposes. Buddhist tradition remembers him as a great patron who built a huge stupa at Peshawar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanishka.jpg" alt="Kanishka" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KanishkaCoin3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
